301
00:14:55.570 --> 00:14:57.150
I always think about that too.
我也总是这么想。

302
00:14:57.150 --> 00:14:58.850
If it's well-written, I have no problem.
如果剧本写得好，我没问题。

303
00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:01.910
And then our joke is we'd never make it on a medical show.
我们常开玩笑说，我们绝对演不了医疗剧。

304
00:15:03.350 --> 00:15:07.650
Because all of the things they have to just like quickly rapid fire off.
因为他们必须快速说出一连串专业术语。

305
00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:10.790
I'd be like, oh no, I'm not, I'm not going to audition for that.
我会想，噢不，我才不去试镜那种角色呢。

306
00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:12.210
It'll be a disaster.
那绝对是一场灾难。

307
00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:12.610
Yeah.
没错。

308
00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:13.710
No, that would be tough.
是啊，那确实很难。

309
00:15:14.150 --> 00:15:22.930
So Steve, we have talked about many times on this podcast, just about our gratitude for
史蒂夫，我们在播客里多次提到过，我们非常感激

310
00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:28.830
your just stability and consistency and your leadership.
你那种稳重、一贯的表现以及你的领导力。

311
00:15:28.830 --> 00:15:35.490
So much so that a fan wrote in Sarah H and said, we've heard how supportive and professional
甚至有位粉丝莎拉·H来信说，我们听说史蒂夫在片场

312
00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:40.410
Steve is on set and I would love to hear Steve's perspective or advice on setting the tone
非常支持大家且极其专业，我很想听听史蒂夫关于如何在工作中

313
00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:42.790
at work and fostering a supportive environment.
定下基调并营造相互支持的环境的看法或建议。

314
00:15:43.150 --> 00:15:45.570
Let's start by saying I am pretty great.
首先得说，我确实很棒。

315
00:15:47.570 --> 00:15:49.550
So some of it's just in your DNA.
所以这部分可能写在你的基因里。

316
00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:52.930
I mean, you had a shirt you would wear that said, I'm fantastic, right?
我是说，你以前穿过一件写着“我很棒”的衬衫，对吧？

317
00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:54.150
That's right.
没错。

318
00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:55.870
A shirt with my face on it.
上面还印着我的脸。

319
00:15:56.050 --> 00:15:58.910
So just to reiterate that it's me.
就是为了重申那个人是我。

320
00:15:59.550 --> 00:16:01.150
That's really nice of you to say.
你能这么说真是太好了。

321
00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:07.550
But I, again, I, I felt like I was part of this very unique ensemble.
但我再次强调，我一直觉得自己是这个独特剧组的一员。

322
00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:11.010
And so I never thought of myself that way.
所以我从没那样看待过自己。

323
00:16:11.210 --> 00:16:17.850
I thought of Greg as our leader and our sort of mentor in terms of this world.
我认为格雷格才是我们的领导者，也是我们在那个世界里的导师。

324
00:16:18.110 --> 00:16:20.490
And he oversaw everything.
他统筹着一切。

325
00:16:21.530 --> 00:16:23.490
And everyone was so nice.
而且每个人都非常好。

326
00:16:23.710 --> 00:16:29.810
Everyone was so kind to each other that I never got a sense that it should be any other
大家对彼此都非常友善，所以我从未觉得事情应该以其他方式进行，

327
00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:37.590
way than the, just in terms of the, the production of the show, like from, from wardrobe to the
就剧集的制作而言，从服装组到

328
00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:44.270
writers, to the, all of the crew, you know, catering, craft service, everybody felt like they
编剧，再到所有工作人员，你知道，餐饮、后勤，每个人都觉得他们

329
00:16:44.270 --> 00:16:45.030
were a part of something.
是其中的一部分。

330
00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:52.530
And the other part of it is I believe sincerely that every one of us knew no, no matter who
另一方面，我真诚地相信我们每个人都知道，无论谁

331
00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:59.250
watched it or whether it had any sort of longevity in terms of viewership, even if, you know, if
在看这部剧，或者它在收视率方面是否能长久，即使，你知道，如果

332
00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:06.210
we got canceled after a season or two or three, I was so, I think we were all so happy and
我们在播出一两季或三季后被砍，我想我们所有人依然会感到非常快乐

333
00:17:06.210 --> 00:17:11.490
thankful for the time that we got to work on the show and to be with one another.
并感激我们能一起工作、彼此相伴的时光。

334
00:17:11.490 --> 00:17:15.010
It was really, it just, it was a special, a special thing.
那真的很特别，那是一段特别的经历。

335
00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:17.870
And, and we were aware of how special it was in the moment.
而且，我们在当时就意识到了它是多么特别。

336
00:17:18.590 --> 00:17:19.350
For sure.
确实。

337
00:17:19.570 --> 00:17:20.090
For sure.
确实。

338
00:17:20.490 --> 00:17:30.710
I always was just super thankful for the writing because it was like, you know, when you do an
我一直非常感激剧本，因为你知道，当你做

339
00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:34.030
improv show or a sketch comedy show or something, and there's one person that wants the joke,
即兴表演或小品喜剧时，如果有一个人总想抢戏、

340
00:17:34.170 --> 00:17:36.850
wants the joke, wants the joke, and they just kill the show.
总想抢戏、总想抢戏，他们会毁了整场演出。

341
00:17:37.250 --> 00:17:39.110
I never felt that on our show.
我在我们的剧里从未有过这种感觉。

342
00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:45.210
It was like, if Phyllis had the best button, she could level the room or Oscar or Creed.
如果菲利斯有最好的包袱，她能镇住全场，奥斯卡或克里德也一样。

343
00:17:45.410 --> 00:17:45.990
It didn't matter.
这不重要。

344
00:17:46.230 --> 00:17:48.290
Like the jokes came from everyone.
笑话来自每个人。

345
00:17:48.490 --> 00:17:50.130
It was a real creative collective.
那是一个真正的创意集体。

346
00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:51.750
And the writers wrote that way.
编剧们也是这样创作的。

347
00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:53.350
And Greg treated us that way.
格雷格也是这样对待我们的。

348
00:17:53.670 --> 00:17:56.230
Like Greg could look at a scene and be like, no, you know what?
比如格雷格看一场戏时会说，不，你知道吗？

349
00:17:56.270 --> 00:17:58.650
It's not working with Jim getting the laugh.
让吉姆来抖包袱效果不好。

350
00:17:59.010 --> 00:18:01.990
Let's flip that and let's have Stanley say it.
我们换一下，让斯坦利来说那句台词。

351
00:18:02.110 --> 00:18:02.250
Right.
没错。

352
00:18:02.410 --> 00:18:03.850
And then it just floored everyone.
然后它就让所有人都惊呆了。

353
00:18:03.850 --> 00:18:05.810
And so I always felt that way.
所以我一直都是这么觉得的。

354
00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:09.470
And I feel like Greg set that tone for us that like everybody gets to play.
我觉得格雷格为我们定下了基调，那就是每个人都有机会表现。

355
00:18:09.670 --> 00:18:11.030
You're exactly right.
你说得太对了。

356
00:18:11.950 --> 00:18:14.050
Everyone got their shot.
每个人都得到了机会。

357
00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:17.670
Not just that, but everyone else loved it.
不仅如此，其他人也都喜欢这样。

358
00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:19.370
Everyone else was supportive of it.
大家都对此表示支持。

359
00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:22.770
There was no resentment, no ego involved.
没有怨恨，也没有自负。

360
00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:25.430
And like, why not?
而且，为什么不呢？

361
00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:26.550
That's the way to do it.
这才是正确的方法。

362
00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:31.810
And I think you can see it when you watch the show because there is a calm to it.
我想当你观看这部剧时就能看出来，因为整部剧有一种从容感。

363
00:18:31.970 --> 00:18:35.350
Like, no one's, everyone, it's all good.
就像，没人会怎样，大家，一切都很好。

364
00:18:35.950 --> 00:18:36.150
Yeah.
是啊。

365
00:18:36.490 --> 00:18:44.530
Everyone's, everyone is, everyone's loving each other, you know, in terms of us as actors and people beyond the characters.
每个人，每个人都在，每个人都互相关爱，你知道，作为演员和角色之外的人来说。

366
00:18:44.850 --> 00:18:48.950
I get so excited for conference room scenes because those were our rapid fire things.
我非常期待会议室的戏份，因为那是我们进行快节奏互动的时候。

367
00:18:48.950 --> 00:18:56.110
I mean, Michael is up there, but literally every single person in the room has either a bit or a look.
我的意思是，迈克尔在上面，但房间里的每一个人都有自己的笑料或表情。

368
00:18:56.130 --> 00:18:58.190
And it's just so fun to watch.
看的时候真的太有趣了。

369
00:18:58.350 --> 00:18:59.770
Almost impossible to get through.
几乎没法顺利拍完。

370
00:19:00.010 --> 00:19:01.450
Oh, and we didn't.
哦，我们确实没拍完。

371
00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:01.950
Oh, my God.
天哪。

372
00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:02.810
We laughed so much.
我们笑得太多了。

373
00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:08.470
Well, people are very excited for you to talk about some of your favorite memories of shooting The Office.
嗯，大家都很期待你谈谈拍摄《办公室》时的一些难忘回忆。

374
00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:14.190
Specifically, they're curious if you have a favorite episode, which I think is like asking, do you have a favorite child?
特别是，他们很好奇你有没有最喜欢的一集，我觉得这就像问你最喜欢哪个孩子一样。

375
00:19:14.350 --> 00:19:15.050
It's very hard.
这太难了。

376
00:19:15.110 --> 00:19:15.510
It's so hard.
真的太难了。

377
00:19:15.790 --> 00:19:17.090
Yeah, that is hard.
是啊，那确实很难。

378
00:19:17.090 --> 00:19:31.670
And, you know, my last one, talk about heart, it was a really difficult episode to do, but I also loved it at the same time because it was sort of that end game for Michael.
而且，你知道，我拍的最后一集，谈到情感，那集拍起来真的很困难，但同时我也很喜欢，因为那是迈克尔的终局。

379
00:19:31.870 --> 00:19:39.330
It was that the culmination, the sort of showing the growth that he didn't need the big send off.
那是高潮，展现了他不需要盛大的告别仪式这种成长。

380
00:19:39.430 --> 00:19:40.690
He didn't need the big party.
他不需要盛大的派对。

381
00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:49.890
He could say goodbye to all of his friends on his own terms without any of the, you know, the fanfare, exactly.
他可以按照自己的方式与所有的朋友告别，不需要任何，你知道，大张旗鼓的排场，确实如此。

382
00:19:50.430 --> 00:19:58.810
And so that, I really enjoyed, you know, having that character evolve to that point in that moment.
所以，我真的很享受让那个角色在那个时刻进化到那种程度。

383
00:19:58.950 --> 00:20:05.710
And people always point to this one, but dinner party, dinner party's up there.
人们总是提到这一集，但《晚宴》那一集确实很棒。

384
00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:11.510
And, you know, we'd come back after the writer's strike, so that was the first thing that we had shot.
而且，你知道，我们在编剧罢工后回来，所以那是我们拍摄的第一件事。

385
00:20:11.650 --> 00:20:17.750
I know you've discussed it, but I think everyone was so ready to get back to work and had really missed each other.
我知道你们讨论过它，但我认为每个人都迫不及待地想回到工作中，而且真的很想念彼此。

386
00:20:17.750 --> 00:20:24.010
And this is an episode that had just been sitting there waiting to happen for a while.
这一集其实已经筹备了很久，等待着拍摄。

387
00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:38.030
And it's so weird and kind of dense thematically and crazy and had great guest appearances and great sight gags.
它非常古怪，主题上很丰富，很疯狂，还有很棒的客串和视觉笑话。

388
00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:41.730
It was also really dark in a lot of ways and very sad.
它在很多方面也非常黑暗，非常悲伤。

389
00:20:41.730 --> 00:20:42.250
Yeah.
是啊。

390
00:20:42.730 --> 00:20:45.030
And kind of emotionally fraught.
而且在情感上很紧张。

391
00:20:45.190 --> 00:20:48.090
So I, there was a lot going on in that one.
所以，那一集里发生了很多事情。

392
00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:49.430
So I like that one too.
所以我也很喜欢那一集。

393
00:20:49.610 --> 00:20:53.190
We didn't realize until we were breaking it down and we did a little Googling.
我们直到拆解剧情并上网搜索时才意识到。

394
00:20:53.610 --> 00:20:56.190
Apparently, it was a huge bomb when it first aired.
显然，它刚播出时反响非常惨淡。

395
00:20:56.490 --> 00:20:58.250
It was not a fan favorite.
它当时并不是粉丝的最爱。

396
00:20:58.630 --> 00:21:00.290
It is not what it is today.
它不像今天这样受欢迎。

397
00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:03.570
But it was, people did not care for it.
但当时，人们并不喜欢它。

398
00:21:03.630 --> 00:21:08.830
The show in general, you know, was a huge bomb when people first saw it.
这部剧总体来说，你知道，刚开始播出时也是惨败。

399
00:21:08.830 --> 00:21:09.490
This is true.
这是真的。

400
00:21:10.090 --> 00:21:11.990
I, you know, acquired taste, I suppose.
我想，这大概是需要时间去品味的吧。

401
00:21:12.490 --> 00:21:16.150
Another one to your point that people did not like at first was Scott's Tots.
关于你提到的另一点，人们起初不喜欢的一集是《斯科特的托特们》。

402
00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:16.450
Yes.
没错。

403
00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:22.590
And I loved doing that episode because it was so horrible.
我非常喜欢拍那一集，因为它太糟糕了。

404
00:21:22.610 --> 00:21:30.130
But I think expressed so much about this guy's persona because his heart definitely in the right place.
但我认为那一集很好地展现了这个人的性格，因为他的初衷绝对是好的。

405
00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:30.290
Yes.
是的。

406
00:21:30.370 --> 00:21:34.230
And a huge heart, but so wrong.
心肠很好，但做法大错特错。

407
00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:35.610
No follow through.
做事没有后续。

408
00:21:35.610 --> 00:21:43.770
And I think there was a talking head, something to the effect of, of all the stupid things I've done in my life.
我记得有个采访镜头，大意是说，在我这辈子做过的所有蠢事里。

409
00:21:44.230 --> 00:21:47.570
This was the most generous or something like that.
这是最慷慨的一件，或者类似的话。

410
00:21:47.930 --> 00:21:48.210
Yes, the most generous.
对，最慷慨的一件。

411
00:21:48.590 --> 00:21:50.290
It was something to that effect.
差不多就是这个意思。

412
00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:55.250
I think it's like of all the empty promises I've made in my life, this one was the most generous.
我想应该是：在我这辈子许下的所有空头支票里，这一张是最慷慨的。

413
00:21:57.450 --> 00:21:58.610
Great memory, Jenna.
记性真好，珍娜。

414
00:21:58.890 --> 00:21:59.490
That's it.
就是那句。

415
00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:02.430
That's just such a hard line to say with a straight face.
那句台词真的很难一本正经地说出来。

416
00:22:03.190 --> 00:22:05.630
You have so many amazing talking heads.
你有很多精彩的采访镜头。

417
00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:07.290
We talk about it every week.
我们每周都会聊到这个。

418
00:22:07.530 --> 00:22:11.710
And I sort of go and look at the deleted scenes and there's some real gems in there too.
我偶尔会去看看删减片段，里面也有一些真正的珍宝。

419
00:22:11.870 --> 00:22:13.770
But Jenna, your favorite is...
不过珍娜，你最喜欢的是……

420
00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:14.550
I have a favorite.
我确实有一个最喜欢的。

421
00:22:14.810 --> 00:22:14.970
Yeah.
是吗。

422
00:22:15.130 --> 00:22:18.290
And we have an audio clip of it so that we can listen to it together.
我们有一段音频剪辑，可以一起听听。

423
00:22:18.550 --> 00:22:18.790
Okay.
好啊。

424
00:22:18.790 --> 00:22:21.790
...is after Michael burns his foot on the Foreman grill.
……是迈克尔在乔治·福尔曼烤架上烫伤脚之后的那段。

425
00:22:21.970 --> 00:22:22.610
Oh, yeah.
哦，对。

426
00:22:22.610 --> 00:22:27.370
And you very angrily explain how it happened.
你非常愤怒地解释了事情的经过。

427
00:22:27.970 --> 00:22:29.430
You're so annoyed.
你当时特别烦躁。

428
00:22:29.650 --> 00:22:30.870
You're so defensive.
你表现得特别防备。

429
00:22:31.250 --> 00:22:33.390
Like, why do I have to explain this?
就像是，为什么我非得解释这个？

430
00:22:33.550 --> 00:22:34.830
But I guess here it goes.
但我想还是说吧。

431
00:22:35.130 --> 00:22:35.630
Again.
再听一遍。

432
00:22:36.150 --> 00:22:37.210
Wait, we got to hear it.
等等，我们得听听。

433
00:22:37.210 --> 00:22:37.350
Yeah.
好。

434
00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:39.430
I enjoy having breakfast in bed.
我喜欢在床上吃早餐。

435
00:22:39.610 --> 00:22:41.490
I like waking up to the smell of bacon.
我喜欢在培根的香味中醒来。

436
00:22:41.690 --> 00:22:41.970
Sue me.
去告我吧。

437
00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:44.910
And since I don't have a butler, I have to do it myself.
因为我没有管家，所以我得自己动手。

438
00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:51.330
So, most nights before I go to bed, I will lay six strips of bacon out on my George Foreman grill.
所以，大多数晚上睡觉前，我都会在乔治·福尔曼烤架上铺六片培根。

439
00:22:51.790 --> 00:22:52.970
Then I go to sleep.
然后我就去睡觉。

440
00:22:53.330 --> 00:22:55.790
When I wake up, I plug in the grill.
醒来时，我插上烤架电源。

441
00:22:56.010 --> 00:22:57.430
I go back to sleep again.
然后我又回去接着睡。

442
00:22:57.870 --> 00:23:01.430
Then I wake up to the smell of crackling bacon.
接着我就在培根滋滋作响的香味中醒来。

443
00:23:02.050 --> 00:23:02.970
It is delicious.
那味道太美了。

444
00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:03.930
It's good for me.
这对我有好处。

445
00:23:04.110 --> 00:23:05.210
It's a perfect way to start the day.
这是开启一天的完美方式。

446
00:23:06.170 --> 00:23:07.350
Today, I got up.
今天，我起床了。

447
00:23:07.510 --> 00:23:10.650
I stepped onto the grill and I clamped down on my foot.
我踩到了烤架上，脚被夹住了。

448
00:23:10.790 --> 00:23:11.150
That's it.
就是这样。

449
00:23:11.290 --> 00:23:13.550
I don't see what's so hard to believe about that.
我不明白这有什么难以置信的。

450
00:23:17.030 --> 00:23:18.770
I love the big sigh.
我喜欢那个长叹声。

451
00:23:19.110 --> 00:23:25.370
After you've explained how you eat bacon, six strips of bacon every morning, you're like, so today.
在你解释完你是怎么吃培根的，每天早上六片培根之后，你就像是说，今天就这样了。

452
00:23:28.180 --> 00:23:33.960
I think my favorite part of that is when he says, and it's good for me.
我觉得那段里我最喜欢的部分是他说的，而且这对我有好处。

453
00:23:35.220 --> 00:23:36.100
Just slipping.
只是顺口溜出来的。

454
00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:38.240
How good?
有多好？

455
00:23:39.020 --> 00:23:42.140
How healthy eating six strips of bacon in the morning.
早上吃六片培根有多健康。

456
00:23:43.040 --> 00:23:43.520
Oh.
噢。

457
00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:46.520
But there's so much about that if you unpack it.
但如果你仔细拆解一下，里面有很多东西。

458
00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:50.100
Like, it actually is really nice to wake up to the smell of bacon.
比如，在培根的香味中醒来确实很美好。

459
00:23:50.340 --> 00:23:52.380
I have that memory from childhood.
我童年就有这样的记忆。

460
00:23:52.640 --> 00:23:52.960
It's comfort.
那是一种慰藉。

461
00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:57.880
You wake up on a Sunday, dad's downstairs making the pancakes, making some bacon.
周日早上醒来，爸爸在楼下做煎饼，煎培根。

462
00:23:58.660 --> 00:24:00.760
It is actually a really, he has a point.
这确实，他说的有道理。

463
00:24:01.040 --> 00:24:02.680
Maybe Jeff made bacon.
也许杰夫做了培根。

464
00:24:02.680 --> 00:24:05.060
He doesn't say it the way you just did, though.
不过他说话的方式可不像你刚才那样。

465
00:24:05.060 --> 00:24:06.400
No, that's the problem.
不，问题就在这儿。

466
00:24:06.700 --> 00:24:19.480
And what was so great about those talking heads and so terrifying in a way was they were so well-written that you just didn't want to screw it up.
那些访谈片段之所以那么棒，又在某种程度上那么可怕，是因为它们写得太好了，你根本不想搞砸。

467
00:24:21.040 --> 00:24:24.740
There was an obligation, and I know you guys felt this too.
有一种责任感，我知道你们也有这种感觉。

468
00:24:24.740 --> 00:24:34.060
Like, when you had a really good line, and it here comes, you would try to do everything you could to take the pedal off a little bit.
比如，当你有一句特别好的台词要说时，你会尽一切努力稍微收着点。

469
00:24:34.280 --> 00:24:37.160
You know, don't floor it for the joke.
你知道，别为了抖包袱而用力过猛。

470
00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:44.720
Really ease off and let it seem like this is just a natural extension of what this guy is thinking or what these women are thinking.
要放轻松，让它看起来就像是这个人或这些女性内心想法的自然延伸。

471
00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:55.000
And that was the hardest part, is to not point to all of these fantastic jokes, to just make them feel conversational, and also just to not laugh.
最难的部分就是不要刻意去强调那些绝妙的笑话，只是让它们听起来像对话，而且还要忍住不笑。

472
00:24:55.360 --> 00:25:00.460
I'm sure I laughed 20 times during that, trying to do that talking head.
我敢肯定我在拍那个访谈片段时笑了20次，一直在努力完成它。

473
00:25:00.460 --> 00:25:07.320
And there's one talking head, I, what was it, that I could barely get through.
有一个访谈片段，我，是什么来着，我几乎没法演完。

474
00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:09.100
And it was the same sort of thing.
也是同样的情况。

475
00:25:09.240 --> 00:25:11.520
It was something so ridiculous.
内容太荒谬了。

476
00:25:11.860 --> 00:25:13.320
There's one in the bloopers.
花絮里有一个。

477
00:25:13.620 --> 00:25:15.280
You know, Isabel's obsessed with the bloopers.
你知道，伊莎贝尔对花絮着迷。

478
00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:16.120
She loves them.
她很喜欢看。

479
00:25:16.260 --> 00:25:18.900
So she'll be like, oh, mom, you're at season seven.
所以她会说，噢，妈妈，你看到第七季了。

480
00:25:19.060 --> 00:25:19.900
There's great bloopers.
那里的花絮很棒。

481
00:25:20.060 --> 00:25:20.880
I'm like, okay, is.
我说，好吧，是啊。

482
00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:26.460
But there was one, Steve, that you could not get through where you said, I'm going to get it wrong.
但有一个片段，史蒂夫，你一直演不下去，你说，我肯定会说错。

483
00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:29.060
But you're like, Michael's like, I'm not a bad guy.
但你就像是，迈克尔说，我不是个坏人。

484
00:25:29.240 --> 00:25:30.460
Sometimes I hit people with my car.
有时候我开车撞人。

485
00:25:30.620 --> 00:25:31.160
That's the one.
就是那个。

486
00:25:31.340 --> 00:25:32.260
That's the one I was thinking.
我刚才想的就是那个。

487
00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:33.920
Yeah, you couldn't get through it.
是啊，你当时一直演不下去。

488
00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:35.200
Sometimes I hit people with my car.
有时候我开车撞人。

489
00:25:35.920 --> 00:25:37.800
Yeah, when I hit Meredith with my car.
对，就是我开车撞了梅雷迪思的时候。

490
00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:41.520
Like trying to absolve myself of having done it.
就像是在试图为自己做过的事开脱。

491
00:25:41.600 --> 00:25:43.140
I'm not a bad guy sometimes.
我不是个坏人，有时候。

492
00:25:43.660 --> 00:25:45.980
I just hit people with my car.
我只是开车撞了人。

493
00:25:46.180 --> 00:25:48.940
And I remember doing it.
我记得当时在演那个。

494
00:25:49.200 --> 00:25:51.280
And I'd be getting close.
我快要演完了。

495
00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:51.800
Yeah.
是啊。

496
00:25:52.360 --> 00:25:55.420
And I couldn't get through it.
但我就是演不下去。

497
00:25:55.420 --> 00:26:02.400
But it was so, it was, it's fun going to know you're going to go into work every day and laugh until you cry.
但那种感觉太棒了，每天去上班都知道自己会笑到流泪，这很有趣。

498
00:26:02.680 --> 00:26:03.020
Yeah.
是啊。

499
00:26:03.160 --> 00:26:10.960
Every day I was on that set, at least one point of the day, I was laughing until tears were coming out of my eyes.
我在那个片场的每一天，每天至少有那么一刻，我都会笑到眼泪直流。

500
00:26:11.080 --> 00:26:12.480
And I mean, what a gift.
我是说，这真是上天赐予的礼物。

501
00:26:12.660 --> 00:26:12.940
Yes.
没错。

502
00:26:13.160 --> 00:26:15.000
Who has a job like that?
谁会有那样的工作？

503
00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:20.280
So Michael Scott had a lot of characters that he would play over the years.
迈克尔·斯科特这些年来扮演过很多角色。

504
00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:24.540
And we found a deleted scene, Steve, that I wanted to share with you.
史蒂夫，我们发现了一个删减片段，我想和你分享一下。

505
00:26:24.540 --> 00:26:25.680
I don't know if you'll remember it.
我不知道你还记不记得。

506
00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:27.340
You know, when you hear it, you'll remember it.
你知道，当你听到的时候，你就会想起来了。

507
00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:29.520
But it's from season three in The Convict.
那是第三季《囚犯》那一集里的。

508
00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:36.880
Michael explains where he got the idea or, I guess, inspiration for all of his characters, in particular, Prison Mike.
迈克尔解释了他所有角色的灵感来源，尤其是“监狱迈克”。

509
00:26:37.260 --> 00:26:38.820
I really wanted you to hear it.
我真的很想让你听听。

510
00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:40.100
Sam, can you play it?
萨姆，能播放一下吗？

511
00:26:41.120 --> 00:27:00.920
When I was just a little kid, we had an assembly at school where a giant owl came out and gave a very impassioned speech about giving hoots and not polluting.
我小时候，学校开过一次集会，一只巨大的猫头鹰出来发表了一番慷慨激昂的演讲，呼吁大家要爱护环境，不要乱扔垃圾。

512
00:27:00.920 --> 00:27:02.560
And you know what?
你知道吗？

513
00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:05.060
I never polluted again.
我再也没有乱扔过垃圾。

514
00:27:05.740 --> 00:27:11.620
It was right then that I realized the power of saying things as a character.
就在那一刻，我意识到了通过角色说话的力量。

515
00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:25.220
People listen to you when you are wearing an elaborate costume or speaking in a voice that is not yours.
当你穿着精致的戏服或用不是你自己的声音说话时，人们才会听你讲。

516
00:27:28.560 --> 00:27:30.420
Can you believe that was deleted?
你能相信这段被删了吗？

517
00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:32.900
That explains Prison Mike.
这解释了“监狱迈克”的由来。

518
00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:34.660
It explains everybody.
这解释了所有角色。

519
00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:36.580
Michael Scarn, anyone.
迈克尔·斯卡恩也是一样。

520
00:27:36.700 --> 00:27:40.860
Anytime you let a conference room meeting in a costume with a voice.
每次你穿着戏服、用变声在会议室开会时。

521
00:27:41.080 --> 00:27:42.100
You thought, this is it.
你心想，就是这样了。

522
00:27:42.200 --> 00:27:43.000
They're going to hear me.
他们会听我说的。

523
00:27:43.100 --> 00:27:44.880
This is how I am most effective.
这是我最有效的方式。

524
00:27:44.880 --> 00:27:45.300
How I get them.
我就是这样搞定他们的。

525
00:27:45.520 --> 00:27:47.540
Because you don't give any hoots anymore.
因为你再也不会乱扔垃圾了。

526
00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:50.760
You give a hoot, you don't pollute.
爱护环境，拒绝污染。

527
00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:57.100
Clearly, this relates back to me tearing apart the plastic, you know, for the dolphins.
显然，这和我为了海豚去撕碎塑料包装有关，你知道的。

528
00:27:57.100 --> 00:27:59.480
Yes, even still, you don't pollute.
没错，即便如此，你还是不能乱扔垃圾。

529
00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:01.220
Steve Carell gave a hoot.
史蒂夫·卡瑞尔确实很爱护环境。

530
00:28:01.440 --> 00:28:02.500
Yeah, he sure did.
没错，他确实是。

531
00:28:04.700 --> 00:28:11.820
Boy, I mean, that's sort of like that vague, foggy, as I was hearing it, I kind of recalled.
天哪，我的意思是，听着这段话，我脑海里那种模糊、朦胧的记忆又浮现出来了。

532
00:28:12.140 --> 00:28:12.500
Yeah.
是啊。

533
00:28:12.980 --> 00:28:15.460
But there are thousands of those.
但这样的片段有成千上万个。

534
00:28:15.460 --> 00:28:25.360
There are, like, different versions of all of this stuff and things that could have made, you know, could made a difference either way.
有很多不同版本的素材，有些片段如果留下来，可能会产生不同的效果。

535
00:28:25.500 --> 00:28:25.780
Yeah.
是啊。

536
00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:26.660
But they were so good.
但它们都太棒了。

537
00:28:26.780 --> 00:28:32.480
You know, the editors and Greg, I never regretted anything that was taken out of the show.
你知道，剪辑师和格雷格，我从不后悔任何被删减的片段。

538
00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:38.260
I, like, had complete confidence that, because you know how some people get possessed of, like, well, why did that scene go?
我完全信任他们，因为你知道有些人会纠结，比如，为什么那场戏被删了？

539
00:28:38.340 --> 00:28:38.620
Right, right.
没错，没错。

540
00:28:38.620 --> 00:28:39.660
What happened there?
那里发生了什么？

541
00:28:39.940 --> 00:28:48.760
They were so good about structuring the show and what needed to be in and what might have been really funny but didn't help.
他们非常擅长把控剧集的结构，知道什么必须保留，什么虽然很好笑但对剧情没帮助。

542
00:28:49.060 --> 00:28:52.020
Or might have explained a whole backstory.
或者有些片段可能会解释整个背景故事。

543
00:28:52.480 --> 00:28:55.620
But they just, they let the audience just jump in the middle.
但他们只是让观众直接进入剧情。

544
00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:57.620
They didn't have to, like, explain everything.
他们不需要解释一切。

545
00:28:57.820 --> 00:29:02.820
But every once in a while as we're doing this rewatch and we find a deleted scene or something that's such a nugget,
但偶尔当我们重温剧集，发现这样一个删减片段或像金子一样的素材时，

546
00:29:02.820 --> 00:29:09.000
we're like, oh, that's why he would come in these costumes and give a big presentation.
我们会想，哦，原来这就是他为什么总是穿着戏服进来做大演讲的原因。

547
00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:10.340
It's because of Hoot.
都是因为“呼呼”猫头鹰。

548
00:29:10.460 --> 00:29:10.720
Sure.
当然。

549
00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:11.220
Hoot the owl.
猫头鹰“呼呼”。

550
00:29:11.800 --> 00:29:14.160
Did you get to give notes on things?
你有机会对这些内容提出意见吗？

551
00:29:14.400 --> 00:29:16.460
Like, were you very much a part of that?
那你当时参与度很高吗？

552
00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:21.960
Like, I would remember sometimes you would stay behind after a table read and they would want to know your thoughts and things.
比如，我记得有时剧本围读结束后，你会留下来，他们想听听你的想法之类的。

553
00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:25.160
They were really collaborative and open in that way, right?
他们在这方面真的很乐于合作，也很开放，对吧？

554
00:29:25.300 --> 00:29:25.740
For sure.
确实。

555
00:29:25.960 --> 00:29:26.400
Yes.
是的。

556
00:29:26.400 --> 00:29:36.760
And the fact that so many of the writers were also actors on the show, there was such a great connection that it didn't feel like different facets of the show.
而且因为很多编剧本身也是剧中的演员，大家联系非常紧密，所以不会觉得剧组分成了不同的派系。

557
00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:40.220
Again, everybody just felt like they were all collaborators.
再说一次，每个人都觉得自己是合作者。

558
00:29:40.600 --> 00:29:50.400
And the writers were part of the ensemble, which I think helped because they understood everyone's voice that much better because they were there and they were hearing it.
编剧也是剧组的一员，我觉得这很有帮助，因为他们就在现场，能亲耳听到大家的表演，所以对每个人的角色语调把握得更好。

559
00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:52.880
But yeah, I mean, I'd weigh in.
不过确实，我是会发表意见。

560
00:29:53.120 --> 00:29:54.220
Would you pitch ideas?
你会提点子吗？

561
00:29:54.560 --> 00:29:54.880
Sure.
会。

562
00:29:55.060 --> 00:29:55.300
Yeah.
是的。

563
00:29:55.300 --> 00:29:56.200
Do you remember any?
还记得什么具体的点子吗？

564
00:29:56.600 --> 00:30:00.980
Well, I remember one specifically, and this was later on.
嗯，我记得一个特别的，那是后期发生的事了。

565
00:30:01.100 --> 00:30:07.980
This was when you and Jim were getting married and they wanted to have a horse go over Niagara Falls.
当时是你和吉姆要结婚了，他们想安排一匹马从尼亚加拉大瀑布上冲下去。

566
00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:08.900
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

567
00:30:08.960 --> 00:30:09.260
Yes.
没错。

568
00:30:10.040 --> 00:30:12.840
And I raised my hand and said, we can't.
我当时举手说，我们不能这么做。

569
00:30:13.680 --> 00:30:14.700
That's crazy.
太疯狂了。

570
00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:18.080
I mean, all due respect, but please don't do that.
我是说，恕我直言，请千万别那么做。

571
00:30:19.740 --> 00:30:21.400
It was not just you.
不只是你一个人反对。

572
00:30:21.400 --> 00:30:27.760
Oh, apparently, when we were breaking down that episode, we talked to Greg about it and he was like, Steve told me not to do it.
哦，显然，当我们拆解那一集剧本时，我们和格雷格聊过，他说史蒂夫也劝我别拍。

573
00:30:28.180 --> 00:30:29.900
Randy told me not to do it.
兰迪也劝我别拍。

574
00:30:30.220 --> 00:30:31.880
You told me not to do it.
你也劝我别拍。

575
00:30:32.180 --> 00:30:34.220
Like, literally everyone told him.
真的，每个人都劝过他。

576
00:30:34.220 --> 00:30:36.280
It was so hard for him to let go of that.
他当时很难放弃那个念头。

577
00:30:37.300 --> 00:30:41.200
Well, I'm sure if it was Greg, it would have been funny.
嗯，我相信如果是格雷格来拍，效果应该会很有趣。

578
00:30:41.620 --> 00:30:41.880
Definitely.
绝对会。

579
00:30:42.740 --> 00:30:51.680
But, and those were rare moments when you felt like, no, I, yeah, I mean, little notes here and there.
不过，那种你需要站出来说“不”的时刻很少，我确实会提一些零星的小建议。

580
00:30:51.680 --> 00:31:02.880
But, again, those writers were so good and so on point about everything that it was more in addition to as opposed to negating.
但话说回来，那些编剧太优秀了，对一切都把握得非常到位，所以我的建议更多是锦上添花，而不是否定他们的想法。

581
00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:03.400
Yeah.
没错。

582
00:31:04.200 --> 00:31:12.780
Side note, did you know, we didn't know this, Randy shared this with us, that up until, like, right, like, the day before, they had a horse.
顺便提一下，你知道吗？我们之前都不知道，是兰迪后来告诉我们的，直到拍摄前一天，他们真的找来了一匹马。

583
00:31:12.780 --> 00:31:16.260
They were training in a big water pool at Paramount Studios.
他们当时在派拉蒙影业的一个大水池里训练那匹马。

584
00:31:17.160 --> 00:31:20.840
Like, it was, they were all systems go, like, right up until, like, the day before.
就是说，一切都准备就绪了，直到拍摄前一天还是这样。

585
00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:21.460
See, that's.
你看，这就是……

586
00:31:21.460 --> 00:31:23.080
There was a horse being trained.
真的有一匹马在接受训练。

587
00:31:23.440 --> 00:31:24.120
Of course there was.
当然会有。

588
00:31:24.460 --> 00:31:29.080
That's what, you don't realize when you write something, somebody starts to build it.
这就是你写剧本时意识不到的问题，一旦写出来，就有人开始着手去造了。

589
00:31:29.180 --> 00:31:29.420
Yeah.
没错。

590
00:31:29.560 --> 00:31:31.000
I mean, it starts to happen.
我是说，事情就是这样开始的。

591
00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:36.780
So, when you say, oh, so now we're going to be out in the woods.
所以当你写下“哦，现在我们要去树林里拍戏了”。

592
00:31:36.980 --> 00:31:37.180
Yeah.
没错。

593
00:31:38.020 --> 00:31:45.260
And then a month later, you're driving an hour and a half up into the Los Angeles forest to shoot a few scenes.
然后一个月后，你就得开车一个半小时去洛杉矶的森林里拍几个镜头。

594
00:31:45.400 --> 00:31:46.860
Like, why did I write this?
当时就会想：我为什么要写这个？

595
00:31:47.200 --> 00:31:48.640
It's a terrible idea.
真是个糟糕的主意。

596
00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:50.620
It's nighttime in the foreSt.
森林里还是大半夜。

597
00:31:50.620 --> 00:31:51.940
It's the middle of nowhere.
荒郊野外的。

598
00:31:52.980 --> 00:31:53.460
Freezing.
冷得要命。

599
00:31:53.700 --> 00:31:54.020
Yeah.
没错。

600
00:31:54.420 --> 00:31:54.880
Terrible.
太糟糕了。

601
00:31:54.880 --> 00:31:55.940
He falls in a creek.
他掉进了一条小溪里。

602
00:31:56.140 --> 00:31:56.380
What?
什么？

603
00:31:57.180 --> 00:31:58.580
Why did I write it?
我为什么要写这个？

604
00:31:58.580 --> 00:32:01.680
So, we've talked how the show was slow to find an audience.
我们之前聊过这部剧起初观众寥寥。

605
00:32:01.680 --> 00:32:05.060
It was sort of like we were doing a little theater production and no one's seeing it.
感觉就像我们在演一场小型舞台剧，却没人来看。

606
00:32:05.160 --> 00:32:08.280
But then one day it turned a corner and everybody knew the show.
但后来有一天，情况出现了转机，大家都知道了这部剧。

607
00:32:08.420 --> 00:32:10.420
And we had people coming up to us.
开始有人主动来找我们搭话了。

608
00:32:10.540 --> 00:32:14.920
Jenna, do you remember we were at an awards show and Laura Dern was like, stop, I have to talk to you.
珍娜，你还记得吗？我们在颁奖典礼上，劳拉·邓恩走过来对我们说，等等，我必须跟你们聊聊。

609
00:32:14.960 --> 00:32:15.440
And we're like, what?
我们当时心想，什么事？

610
00:32:15.740 --> 00:32:16.920
She was like, I love your show.
她说，我太喜欢你们的剧了。

611
00:32:17.020 --> 00:32:18.560
And I was like, this is Laura Dern.
我当时心想，这可是劳拉·邓恩啊。

612
00:32:18.740 --> 00:32:19.460
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

613
00:32:19.660 --> 00:32:25.800
But was there anyone like that for you, Steve, that sort of surprised you and caught you off guard that was a fan of the show?
史蒂夫，对你来说有没有这样的人，让你感到惊讶或措手不及，而且还是这部剧的粉丝？

614
00:32:26.260 --> 00:32:26.920
Laura Dern.
劳拉·邓恩。

615
00:32:27.720 --> 00:32:28.360
Same thing.
也是一样的情况。

616
00:32:28.580 --> 00:32:31.180
I met her somewhere down the road.
后来我在别的地方遇见了她。

617
00:32:31.340 --> 00:32:34.060
I was doing a promotional thing with her.
当时我和她一起做宣传活动。

618
00:32:34.800 --> 00:32:38.120
And I wasn't even aware of your encounter with her.
我甚至都不知道你之前和她有过那次接触。

619
00:32:38.300 --> 00:32:43.500
Because she was clearly a fan because she was very excited to talk about the show.
因为她显然是粉丝，她聊起这部剧时非常兴奋。

620
00:32:43.720 --> 00:32:45.580
And, you know, she said the same thing to me.
而且，你知道，她也对我说了同样的话。

621
00:32:45.680 --> 00:32:46.260
She loved it.
她很喜欢这部剧。

622
00:32:47.520 --> 00:32:53.200
And it's nice when anyone likes something that you're a part of.
当有人喜欢你参与的作品时，感觉总是很棒。

623
00:32:53.200 --> 00:33:01.900
And especially nice when somebody that you respect like that and has sort of a critical eye for things likes your show.
尤其是当你尊敬的人，而且那种眼光独到的人也喜欢你的剧时，感觉就更好了。

624
00:33:02.060 --> 00:33:03.220
So, yeah, it was really nice.
所以，是的，真的很棒。

625
00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:05.360
It was nice hearing any of it, you know.
听到这些评价都很开心，你知道的。

626
00:33:06.420 --> 00:33:07.540
Because you're right.
因为你说得对。

627
00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:09.700
Early on, it felt like we were in a vacuum.
早期的时候，感觉我们就像是在真空里一样。

628
00:33:10.660 --> 00:33:14.600
And is any, let alone appreciating, is anyone watching it?
根本不知道有没有人在看，更别提欣赏了。

629
00:33:14.620 --> 00:33:14.800
Yeah.
是啊。

630
00:33:14.860 --> 00:33:17.700
Is anyone, you know, paying any sort of attention?
不知道有没有人在关注。

631
00:33:17.960 --> 00:33:22.240
And the only part of it that mattered to me was the continuation of the show.
对我来说，唯一重要的事情就是这部剧能继续拍下去。

632
00:33:22.240 --> 00:33:27.960
So, I wanted to have enough people watch it so they kept letting us do it.
所以我只希望有足够多的人看，这样他们才会让我们继续做下去。

633
00:33:28.060 --> 00:33:30.760
Because I didn't really care about the acclaim.
因为我其实并不在乎那些赞誉。

634
00:33:30.960 --> 00:33:31.580
Right, right.
没错，没错。

635
00:33:31.580 --> 00:33:35.900
Or, like, we knew that we liked it and we were having fun with it.
或者说，我们知道自己喜欢这部剧，而且拍得很开心。

636
00:33:35.960 --> 00:33:39.980
And I think we all felt like it was special in that moment.
我觉得我们当时都觉得这部剧很特别。

637
00:33:39.980 --> 00:33:41.780
And that's what mattered to me.
那才是我真正在乎的。

638
00:33:42.660 --> 00:33:43.460
But, yeah.
不过，确实。

639
00:33:43.760 --> 00:33:44.060
Yeah.
是啊。

640
00:33:44.140 --> 00:33:45.300
It was Laura Dern.
就是劳拉·邓恩。

641
00:33:45.560 --> 00:33:50.500
I remember that time early on because we, a few of us had gotten on MySpace.
我记得早期那段时间，因为我们当中有几个人注册了MySpace。

642
00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:57.220
And we were able to interact with the, like, 17 people who were watching our show in the beginning.
我们得以和那最初看剧的17个人互动。

643
00:33:57.460 --> 00:33:59.100
And they were such fans.
他们真的是铁杆粉丝。

644
00:33:59.100 --> 00:34:00.220
And they so got it.
他们完全看懂了。

645
00:34:00.300 --> 00:34:01.300
And they so appreciated it.
他们非常欣赏这部剧。

646
00:34:01.360 --> 00:34:04.340
And that really, like, kind of, that just felt so good, you know.
那种感觉真的特别好，你知道吗。

647
00:34:04.420 --> 00:34:08.920
Because when you make a television show, you don't hear the people laugh when they watch it.
因为当你制作一部电视剧时，你听不到观众看剧时的笑声。

648
00:34:08.920 --> 00:34:14.620
And you don't know how it's being received or a movie or whatever, I guess, unless you sneak in the theater.
你不知道观众的反响如何，电影或其他作品也一样，除非你偷偷溜进电影院。

649
00:34:14.760 --> 00:34:17.520
But you can't, like, go sneak in people's living rooms while they watch it.
但你总不能溜进别人家里看他们看剧吧。

650
00:34:17.820 --> 00:34:23.620
So, I always loved when someone told us that they loved it, especially a fellow artiSt.
所以我一直很喜欢有人告诉我们他们很喜欢这部剧，尤其是同行艺术家。

651
00:34:24.340 --> 00:34:30.540
And they were excited, too, because the show was different with the documentary style and the looking at the camera.
他们也很兴奋，因为这部剧的纪录片风格和直视镜头的拍摄手法与众不同。

652
00:34:30.680 --> 00:34:33.460
Would you look into the camera after you got jobs on The Office?
你们在得到《办公室》的角色后，会忍不住看向镜头吗？

653
00:34:33.720 --> 00:34:37.800
I have never had a job where I didn't look into the camera at some point.
我还没接过哪份工作是完全不需要看向镜头的。

654
00:34:38.200 --> 00:34:38.640
Same.
我也是。

655
00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:41.560
I can't not make the camera a character now.
我现在已经没法不把镜头当成一个角色了。

656
00:34:41.700 --> 00:34:43.160
It's so, such a habit.
这已经成了一种习惯。

657
00:34:43.260 --> 00:34:44.500
At least once.
至少会有一次。

658
00:34:44.740 --> 00:34:44.880
Yeah.
是啊。

659
00:34:45.260 --> 00:34:52.580
I'm sure on Foxcatcher, I, like, looked into the camera and went, nope, you cannot comment on what you just did.
我敢肯定在拍《狐狸猎手》时，我肯定也看向了镜头，然后心想，不行，你不能对你刚才做的事发表评论。

660
00:34:52.740 --> 00:34:53.220
Yeah.
没错。

661
00:34:53.380 --> 00:34:55.020
Because that's not this show.
因为那不是这部剧的风格。

662
00:34:55.420 --> 00:34:55.680
Yeah.
是啊。

663
00:34:56.080 --> 00:34:56.920
Same for me.
我也是一样。

664
00:34:57.060 --> 00:35:00.300
I actually had a director say, Angela, you just looked right down the barrel.
其实有位导演曾对我说：安吉拉，你刚才直勾勾地盯着镜头看了。

665
00:35:00.900 --> 00:35:02.420
I was like, oh, I'm sorry.
我就说：哦，抱歉。

666
00:35:03.260 --> 00:35:04.660
I looked to the left or right.
我当时看向了左边或右边。

667
00:35:04.660 --> 00:35:08.180
It was such a fun kind of discipline.
这真是一种有趣的训练。

668
00:35:08.400 --> 00:35:11.620
Know who was really good at that right off the bat was Amy AdaMs.
你知道谁一开始就特别擅长这个吗？艾米·亚当斯。

669
00:35:12.140 --> 00:35:19.680
I'll never forget because it was kind of a learned strength, I think, on the show to figure out the right way to play the camera,
我永远不会忘记，因为我觉得在剧中掌握如何正确地与镜头互动是一种习得的能力，

670
00:35:19.840 --> 00:35:27.040
to invite the camera in and to make it feel like these people aren't used to the cameras.
要学会邀请镜头进入场景，并让观众觉得这些人并不习惯被拍摄。

671
00:35:27.040 --> 00:35:30.800
So, you couldn't be too savvy, especially at firSt.
所以，你不能表现得太精明，尤其是在刚开始的时候。

672
00:35:30.960 --> 00:35:33.020
You couldn't play it too well.
你不能演得太老练。

673
00:35:33.360 --> 00:35:38.820
You needed to have an awareness of it without, like, having an over-awareness of it.
你需要对镜头有意识，但又不能表现得太刻意。

674
00:35:38.960 --> 00:35:39.200
Yes.
没错。

675
00:35:39.840 --> 00:35:43.520
But I remember when Amy Adams came in to do Purse Girl.
但我记得艾米·亚当斯来演“手提包女孩”的时候。

676
00:35:43.700 --> 00:35:44.120
Was that it?
是那个角色吗？

677
00:35:44.560 --> 00:35:45.300
Hot Girl.
“辣妹”。

678
00:35:45.480 --> 00:35:45.880
Hot Girl.
“辣妹”。

679
00:35:46.100 --> 00:35:46.900
She was selling purses.
她在卖手提包。

680
00:35:46.900 --> 00:35:47.360
Selling the purses.
卖那些手提包。

681
00:35:47.420 --> 00:35:48.020
Selling purses, yeah.
卖手提包，没错。

682
00:35:48.320 --> 00:35:55.540
And I remember she just had, like, this – she was right up to speed with everybody else in terms of how to play the camera.
我记得她当时就——她在如何与镜头互动方面，很快就跟上了大家的节奏。

683
00:35:55.700 --> 00:35:57.420
Like, it's eavesdropping.
就像是在偷听一样。

684
00:35:57.420 --> 00:36:01.480
I don't know how I should react to it.
我不知道该如何对此做出反应。

685
00:36:01.780 --> 00:36:06.460
And that sort of discomfort built in that we had learned how to do.
那种我们已经学会如何表现的内在不适感。

686
00:36:06.820 --> 00:36:08.320
So, she was really good at that.
所以，她在这方面真的很棒。

687
00:36:08.320 --> 00:36:12.360
And that was such an interesting – and we'll never get to do something like that again.
那真是一次有趣的经历——我们以后再也不会有机会做这样的事了。

688
00:36:12.360 --> 00:36:23.040
It was a really interesting acting exercise to be, you know, you're doing – you're in your character, and your character is also being watched.
这是一种非常有趣的表演练习，你知道，你既要扮演你的角色，而你的角色同时又在被监视。

689
00:36:23.480 --> 00:36:29.040
So, there was, like, another layer of something going on with all of these characters, which is kind of cool.
所以，所有这些角色身上都多了一层东西，这真的很酷。

690
00:36:29.460 --> 00:36:31.040
You mentioned Amy AdaMs.
你提到了艾米·亚当斯。

691
00:36:31.480 --> 00:36:35.880
One of my favorite guest stars that we had in terms of chemistry with you was Tim Meadows.
在与你的化学反应方面，我最喜欢的客串明星之一是蒂姆·梅多斯。

692
00:36:36.380 --> 00:36:38.680
You guys doing the baby back ribs.
你们一起吃烤猪肋排的那段。

693
00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:43.400
That also – that episode has one of my favorite lines in it, which is, Gould is dead.
那集里还有我最喜欢的台词之一，就是“古尔德死了”。

694
00:36:44.280 --> 00:36:46.980
Because Jan is no longer Jan Levinson Gould.
因为简不再是简·莱文森·古尔德了。

695
00:36:47.260 --> 00:36:47.520
Yeah.
是啊。

696
00:36:47.520 --> 00:36:50.360
And she's like, no, Michael, we got – we're separated.
她说：不，迈克尔，我们已经——我们分居了。

697
00:36:50.540 --> 00:36:51.180
We're divorced.
我们离婚了。

698
00:36:51.380 --> 00:36:52.180
Gould didn't die.
古尔德没死。

699
00:36:52.260 --> 00:36:57.240
As if – as if she would drop the Gould from her name if he died.
就好像——就好像如果他死了，她就会把古尔德这个姓从名字里去掉一样。

700
00:36:57.500 --> 00:36:57.720
Right.
没错。

701
00:36:58.000 --> 00:36:58.640
As a widow.
作为一名遗孀。

702
00:36:59.200 --> 00:36:59.440
Yeah.
是啊。

703
00:36:59.680 --> 00:37:00.080
Amazing.
太棒了。

704
00:37:00.080 --> 00:37:02.660
But, yeah, I love you guys.
不过，说真的，我爱你们大家。

705
00:37:02.840 --> 00:37:09.560
I don't know how you got through all the rib-eating and singing and comedy from that episode.
我不知道你们是怎么熬过那一集里所有的吃排骨、唱歌和喜剧表演的。

706
00:37:09.660 --> 00:37:11.920
I've known Tim for a long time, too.
我也认识蒂姆很久了。

707
00:37:11.920 --> 00:37:16.960
And we did a Second City show that went to Washington, D.C.
我们曾一起做过一场“第二城市”的演出，还去了华盛顿特区。

708
00:37:17.200 --> 00:37:19.380
at the Kennedy Center with Nancy.
在肯尼迪中心，和南希一起。

709
00:37:19.660 --> 00:37:28.380
We went as this group one summer and spent, I don't know, three weeks at the Kennedy Center doing a Second City show.
那个夏天我们作为一个团队去了那里，待了大概三周，在肯尼迪中心表演“第二城市”的节目。

710
00:37:28.500 --> 00:37:33.020
And I had known him from Second City as well, you know, earlier on stage.
而且我更早之前在“第二城市”的舞台上就认识他了。

711
00:37:33.020 --> 00:37:40.540
And he is so – I think he's one of those people that is always great.
他真的非常——我觉得他是那种一直都很棒的人。

712
00:37:40.840 --> 00:37:42.660
He's never not great.
他从没掉过链子。

713
00:37:43.100 --> 00:37:45.940
You know, sometimes people like that are under the radar.
你知道，有时候像这样的人总是默默无闻。

714
00:37:46.440 --> 00:37:49.380
And then they pop out and you say, oh, my gosh.
然后他们突然冒出来，你会惊叹，天哪。

715
00:37:49.540 --> 00:37:51.400
Like, they're incredible.
简直不可思议。

716
00:37:52.260 --> 00:37:55.200
And, yeah, he's, I think, one of the beSt.
而且，没错，我觉得他是最棒的人之一。

717
00:37:55.240 --> 00:37:56.600
And he's a great improviser.
他是个很棒的即兴表演者。

718
00:37:56.840 --> 00:38:00.040
And he's so – he's so effortless.
而且他非常——他非常轻松自如。

719
00:38:00.040 --> 00:38:03.600
He just doesn't look like he's ever breaking a sweat.
他看起来从不费吹灰之力。

720
00:38:03.800 --> 00:38:04.840
And he's hilarious.
而且他非常搞笑。

721
00:38:05.880 --> 00:38:07.080
Yeah, I'm a big fan.
是啊，我是他的头号粉丝。

722
00:38:07.220 --> 00:38:07.840
He's a great guy.
他是个很棒的人。

723
00:38:09.080 --> 00:38:10.780
Well, why don't we take a break?
好吧，我们休息一下好吗？

724
00:38:10.780 --> 00:38:13.820
And when we come back, we have some more questions from the fans.
回来之后，我们还有一些粉丝提问。

725
00:38:14.040 --> 00:38:14.360
Okie do.
好嘞。

726
00:38:23.290 --> 00:38:24.250
All right, we're back.
好了，我们回来了。

727
00:38:24.430 --> 00:38:33.810
And while we're on break, before we get to fan questions, Steve, I remembered this cold open that we did that led into a talking head that was one sentence from you.
在休息的时候，在回答粉丝提问之前，史蒂夫，我记起我们拍过一个开场戏，紧接着是一个你的单人访谈，只有一句话。

728
00:38:34.350 --> 00:38:36.850
When I rewatched it, I laughed so hard.
重看的时候，我笑得好厉害。

729
00:38:36.850 --> 00:38:38.730
I had tears streaming down my face.
笑得我眼泪都流出来了。

730
00:38:39.430 --> 00:38:44.610
Do you remember the cold open that we did where we're all trying to take the Christmas photo?
你还记得我们拍的那场大家都在努力拍圣诞合影的开场戏吗？

731
00:38:45.130 --> 00:38:45.650
Yes.
记得。

732
00:38:46.210 --> 00:38:46.690
Yes.
记得。

733
00:38:46.690 --> 00:38:46.750
Yeah.
是啊。

734
00:38:47.090 --> 00:38:51.870
And we're trying to jump on three, three, two, one, one, two, three.
我们当时在试着一起跳起来，喊着三、三、二、一、一、二、三。

735
00:38:52.230 --> 00:38:52.410
Yeah.
对。

736
00:38:52.570 --> 00:38:53.510
This whole thing.
就是那整件事。

737
00:38:53.610 --> 00:38:55.270
Dwight's like, Phyllis didn't jump.
德怀特说，菲利斯没跳。

738
00:38:55.410 --> 00:38:56.290
Angela didn't jump.
安吉拉没跳。

739
00:38:56.430 --> 00:38:56.650
Yes.
没错。

740
00:38:57.190 --> 00:38:59.450
And it's so long, this cold open.
而且那段开场戏真的很长。

741
00:38:59.450 --> 00:39:02.330
And then it cuts to a talking head of Michael.
然后切到了迈克尔的单人访谈。

742
00:39:02.550 --> 00:39:04.690
And Michael says, we never got it.
迈克尔说，我们从来没拍成过。

743
00:39:06.330 --> 00:39:07.530
I, you know what?
我，你知道吗？

744
00:39:07.710 --> 00:39:14.150
That, that cold open is a perfect demonstration of us as an ensemble.
那段开场戏完美展示了我们作为一个剧组的配合。

745
00:39:14.150 --> 00:39:14.770
I think.
我是这么认为的。

746
00:39:14.990 --> 00:39:15.150
Yeah.
没错。

747
00:39:15.550 --> 00:39:22.890
Because to, to make it look as bad as it did, took some effort.
因为要拍出那种糟糕的效果，其实是需要下功夫的。

748
00:39:23.090 --> 00:39:23.410
It did.
确实。

749
00:39:23.590 --> 00:39:26.970
Like, we had to be completely in sync to be that out of sync.
就像是，我们必须完全同步才能表现出那种完全不同步的效果。

750
00:39:27.170 --> 00:39:27.370
Yeah.
是啊。

751
00:39:28.250 --> 00:39:30.490
And everyone jumping at different times.
每个人跳的时间都不一样。

752
00:39:30.730 --> 00:39:30.910
Yeah.
是啊。

753
00:39:31.470 --> 00:39:33.690
And Michael getting frustrated like a dad would.
迈克尔像个老爸一样感到沮丧。

754
00:39:33.810 --> 00:39:34.790
Like, come on, come on, guys.
就像是，拜托，拜托，伙计们。

755
00:39:35.310 --> 00:39:39.730
I think that's a perfect example of, of how in sync we were as a caSt.
我觉得这是一个完美的例子，说明我们作为演员配合得有多默契。

756
00:39:39.850 --> 00:39:41.830
Because we all got what that thing was.
因为我们都明白那件事是什么。

757
00:39:42.010 --> 00:39:42.170
Yeah.
是啊。

758
00:39:42.270 --> 00:39:44.350
And how many people needed to be going at one time.
以及有多少人需要同时行动。

759
00:39:44.510 --> 00:39:45.910
But we didn't talk about it.
但我们并没有讨论过。

760
00:39:45.990 --> 00:39:46.230
No.
没有。

761
00:39:46.370 --> 00:39:50.110
We didn't discuss like, well, on the count of three, you two are going to go.
我们没有商量说，好吧，数到三，你们两个一起上。

762
00:39:50.290 --> 00:39:53.790
And then we just, as a group, we just felt that.
我们只是作为一个团队，凭感觉做到了。

763
00:39:54.010 --> 00:39:56.050
And not to give ourselves too big a pat on the back.
并不是要自吹自擂。

764
00:39:56.190 --> 00:40:01.970
But, but I think I've, I've watched that since and thought the same thing.
但我后来重看的时候，也觉得确实如此。

765
00:40:01.970 --> 00:40:05.610
Like, that's, there was something, something cool happening there.
就是说，当时确实发生了一些很酷的事情。

766
00:40:05.770 --> 00:40:08.970
That was one of my favorite group cold opens for that reason.
正因如此，那是我最喜欢的集体开场戏之一。

767
00:40:09.190 --> 00:40:09.610
I know.
我知道。

768
00:40:09.890 --> 00:40:11.670
Well, let's get into these fan questions.
好了，让我们来看看这些粉丝提问。

769
00:40:11.910 --> 00:40:11.960
Okay.
好的。

770
00:40:12.430 --> 00:40:13.130
Catherine G.
凯瑟琳·G。

771
00:40:13.350 --> 00:40:14.030
and Christy R.
和克里斯蒂·R。

772
00:40:14.130 --> 00:40:14.710
both asked,
都问到，

773
00:40:14.850 --> 00:40:18.170
if you had to choose a different character to play on The Office besides Michael,
如果除了迈克尔之外，你必须选择扮演《办公室》里的另一个角色，

774
00:40:18.310 --> 00:40:19.690
is there one you'd like to be?
你会想演哪一个？

775
00:40:20.010 --> 00:40:20.450
Wow.
哇。

776
00:40:20.650 --> 00:40:24.490
I can't imagine anyone but the people who played their parts.
除了现在的演员，我无法想象其他人来演这些角色。

777
00:40:25.070 --> 00:40:29.790
Like, right off the top of my head, I think Dwight is a very fun role to play.
比如，我第一时间想到的是，我觉得德怀特是一个非常有趣的角色。

778
00:40:29.790 --> 00:40:34.030
But, I would have been terrible.
但是，我肯定演得很烂。

779
00:40:34.550 --> 00:40:36.750
I mean, Rain was perfect for that part.
我是说，雷恩简直是那个角色的不二人选。

780
00:40:37.970 --> 00:40:41.810
So, no, I never, like, I never imagined myself in another part.
所以，不，我从来没有想象过自己演其他角色。

781
00:40:42.190 --> 00:40:44.150
It was, it was just as it should have been.
一切都恰如其分。

782
00:40:44.870 --> 00:40:45.550
Lauren D.
劳伦·D。

783
00:40:45.770 --> 00:40:46.210
asked,
问到，

784
00:40:46.390 --> 00:40:50.930
what trend or big pop culture moment that's happened since 2011,
自2011年以来发生的流行趋势或重大流行文化事件中，

785
00:40:51.510 --> 00:40:53.910
do you think Michael would have gotten obsessed about?
你觉得迈克尔会沉迷于哪一个？

786
00:40:53.910 --> 00:40:58.670
For example, Lauren thinks Michael would have been all over the ice bucket challenge.
例如，劳伦认为迈克尔一定会热衷于冰桶挑战。

787
00:40:58.830 --> 00:40:59.510
Oh, yeah.
哦，没错。

788
00:41:00.030 --> 00:41:02.290
I think, I think she's absolutely right.
我觉得她说得太对了。

789
00:41:02.410 --> 00:41:03.170
So much, so much ice water.
会有那么多的冰水。

790
00:41:03.350 --> 00:41:07.150
Oh, and would have, would have done something wrong.
哦，而且他肯定会把事情搞砸。

791
00:41:07.630 --> 00:41:09.650
Someone might have ended up in the hospital.
可能会有人因此进了医院。

792
00:41:10.530 --> 00:41:11.290
Probably Meredith.
大概是梅雷迪思。

793
00:41:12.230 --> 00:41:12.710
Yeah.
是啊。

794
00:41:12.930 --> 00:41:14.550
Dwight would have overdone it, too.
德怀特也会做得太过火。

795
00:41:15.170 --> 00:41:15.650
Yes.
是的。

796
00:41:17.210 --> 00:41:19.450
Yeah, it would have gone south somehow.
是啊，总会以某种方式搞砸的。

797
00:41:19.630 --> 00:41:20.470
It would have gone wrong.
肯定会出岔子的。

798
00:41:20.470 --> 00:41:25.070
So, we say that we think that Michael probably would have made us all learn TikTok dances.
所以，我们认为迈克尔可能会强迫我们所有人去学TikTok舞蹈。

799
00:41:25.610 --> 00:41:25.870
Oh, sure.
哦，当然。

800
00:41:25.870 --> 00:41:27.210
So many TikTok dances.
会有那么多的TikTok舞蹈。

801
00:41:27.530 --> 00:41:27.670
Yeah.
是啊。

802
00:41:27.930 --> 00:41:33.510
Well, the internet wasn't as, certainly, as, as much of a thing.
嗯，当时互联网确实还没那么普及，没那么重要。

803
00:41:34.190 --> 00:41:35.430
Social media, really.
社交媒体也是。

804
00:41:35.830 --> 00:41:36.130
Yeah.
是啊。

805
00:41:36.530 --> 00:41:38.450
We had flip phones when we started the show.
我们刚开始做这档节目时还在用翻盖手机。

806
00:41:38.570 --> 00:41:39.690
Then we got BlackBerrys.
后来我们换成了黑莓手机。

807
00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:41.070
And then at the end, we had iPhones.
最后我们用上了iPhone。

808
00:41:41.410 --> 00:41:41.490
Like.
就像是。

809
00:41:41.710 --> 00:41:42.010
Yeah.
是啊。

810
00:41:42.250 --> 00:41:42.630
Yeah.
是啊。

811
00:41:42.790 --> 00:41:43.930
A lot, a lot changed.
变化真的很大，非常大。

812
00:41:43.930 --> 00:41:50.970
Yeah, there are, there are tons of things that Michael would have glommed on to in, in the worst possible way.
没错，有很多很多事情，迈克尔肯定会以最糟糕的方式沉迷其中。

813
00:41:53.510 --> 00:41:54.390
Beth M.
贝丝·M。

814
00:41:54.630 --> 00:41:59.810
writes in and says, what is the most common Michael line that people quote back to you?
来信问，大家最常引用哪句迈克尔的台词来跟你开玩笑？

815
00:41:59.930 --> 00:42:01.110
What do you think it is?
你觉得是哪句？

816
00:42:01.350 --> 00:42:01.750
Yeah, Beth.
是啊，贝丝。

817
00:42:01.930 --> 00:42:03.250
That's what she said.
就是那句“那是她说的”。

818
00:42:04.570 --> 00:42:06.650
Is there one other than that?
除了那句还有别的吗？

819
00:42:10.020 --> 00:42:13.080
That's the one, that's the one, that's the one I hear.
就是那句，就是那句，我听到的就是那句。

820
00:42:13.360 --> 00:42:13.640
Yeah.
是啊。

821
00:42:13.640 --> 00:42:21.640
And sometimes, I don't know, do people ever stop you and say, oh, could you talk to, can you do a shout out, you know, into my phone?
有时候，我不知道，有没有人拦住你说，噢，你能对着我的手机录段视频吗？

822
00:42:21.840 --> 00:42:22.100
Yeah.
有啊。

823
00:42:22.500 --> 00:42:23.520
And I'm happy to do it.
我很乐意配合。

824
00:42:23.560 --> 00:42:25.860
But when they say, can you say that?
但当他们说，你能说那句台词吗？

825
00:42:25.860 --> 00:42:28.700
I can't, I just, I don't, I can't.
我做不到，我就是，我不行，我真的说不出口。

826
00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:30.780
It can be taken out of context.
那可能会被断章取义。

827
00:42:31.180 --> 00:42:33.420
Please don't make Steve say that's what she said.
请别让史蒂夫说“那是她说的”。

828
00:42:33.660 --> 00:42:35.060
If you see him at the airport.
如果你在机场见到他。

829
00:42:36.080 --> 00:42:38.500
Do you ever, that's what she said in your life.
你生活中会说“那是她说的”吗？

830
00:42:38.660 --> 00:42:40.140
Do you ever find, never?
你从来都不说吗？

831
00:42:40.380 --> 00:42:40.740
No.
从来不说。

832
00:42:41.020 --> 00:42:41.460
Really?
真的吗？

833
00:42:41.700 --> 00:42:42.340
I don't do it.
我从不说。

834
00:42:42.340 --> 00:42:44.240
I, I do throw them out.
我，我会偶尔说。

835
00:42:44.320 --> 00:42:44.760
Jenna does.
珍娜会说。

836
00:42:45.020 --> 00:42:45.620
I do.
我会说。

837
00:42:45.980 --> 00:42:46.340
Yeah.
是啊。

838
00:42:46.580 --> 00:42:47.480
Well, you can.
嗯，你可以说。

839
00:42:47.780 --> 00:42:49.060
You, you can.
你，你可以说。

840
00:42:49.320 --> 00:42:51.540
Gosh, it's gonna be so weird when we give you your parting gift.
天哪，等我们送你离别礼物时一定会很尴尬。

841
00:42:51.640 --> 00:42:53.640
It's a, it's a t-shirt that says that's what she said.
那是一件印着“那是她说的”的T恤。

842
00:42:54.140 --> 00:42:54.580
Wow.
哇。

843
00:42:54.940 --> 00:42:55.700
You'll love it.
你会喜欢的。

844
00:42:55.720 --> 00:42:56.960
I do wear that all the time.
我确实经常穿那件衣服。

845
00:42:56.980 --> 00:42:58.920
And then on the back it says, I'm fantastic.
背面还写着，我棒极了。

846
00:43:00.060 --> 00:43:00.820
Same brain.
心有灵犀。

847
00:43:02.200 --> 00:43:07.320
We really liked this question because we both come from a long line of teachers.
我们很喜欢这个问题，因为我们俩都出身于教师世家。

848
00:43:07.320 --> 00:43:10.300
Someone wanted to know if you remember a favorite teacher.
有人想知道你是否还记得最喜欢的老师。

849
00:43:10.640 --> 00:43:15.320
There was one in particular, a guy named Mr. Blackman, my second grade teacher.
确实有一位，那个人叫布莱克曼先生，是我二年级的老师。

850
00:43:16.620 --> 00:43:20.880
And he's the one who got me interested in acting in the first place.
正是他让我最初对表演产生了兴趣。

851
00:43:20.880 --> 00:43:25.080
Like we were doing like a pilgrims meet the Native Americans thing.
就像我们在演那种清教徒遇见美洲原住民的戏码。

852
00:43:25.400 --> 00:43:27.000
It was a long time ago.
那是很久以前的事了。

853
00:43:27.200 --> 00:43:30.120
So a lot was being glossed over for sure.
所以很多细节肯定都被忽略了。

854
00:43:30.420 --> 00:43:36.020
But what I remember is we were rowing canoes and it was all mime.
但我记得我们当时在划独木舟，全程都是默剧表演。

855
00:43:36.140 --> 00:43:38.700
You know, we were sitting in little chairs in the classroom.
你知道，我们就是坐在教室里的小椅子上。

856
00:43:38.900 --> 00:43:39.720
It wasn't a big play.
那不是什么大型演出。

857
00:43:39.900 --> 00:43:41.320
It was just like an in-class thing.
只是课堂上的一个小活动。

858
00:43:42.200 --> 00:43:45.220
And I started rowing the canoe, my mime rowing.
我开始划独木舟，用默剧的方式划。

859
00:43:45.340 --> 00:43:48.360
And then I changed my hands with the oar and rowed on the other side.
然后我换了手拿桨，划向另一边。

860
00:43:48.640 --> 00:43:49.740
I'll never forget it.
我永远不会忘记那一刻。

861
00:43:49.800 --> 00:43:50.880
He singled me out.
他专门点名表扬了我。

862
00:43:51.000 --> 00:43:53.020
It's the first time I'd ever been singled out in any way.
那是我第一次以任何方式被特别关注。

863
00:43:54.140 --> 00:43:59.880
And he gave me a pat on the back and said, notice how Steve rowed on both sides of the
他拍了拍我的背说，大家注意看史蒂夫是怎么在独木舟的

864
00:43:59.880 --> 00:44:01.620
canoe so we wouldn't go in a circle.
两边划桨的，这样我们就不会原地打转了。

865
00:44:02.040 --> 00:44:07.060
And he kept the canoe straight, you know, in this mime, you know, miming the canoe.
他让独木舟保持直线前进，你知道，就是在这种默剧中，模拟划独木舟。

866
00:44:07.060 --> 00:44:10.060
And something about that really struck me.
那件事真的触动了我。

867
00:44:10.160 --> 00:44:15.540
And I thought, and apart from that, he was just the most lovely guy and really kind to
我想，而且除此之外，他真的是个最可爱的人，对所有的孩子和每个人

868
00:44:15.540 --> 00:44:16.720
all the kids and everyone.
都非常友善。

869
00:44:16.920 --> 00:44:17.540
It was fun.
那很有趣。

870
00:44:17.720 --> 00:44:19.260
It was really just a fun class.
那真是一堂有趣的课。

871
00:44:19.580 --> 00:44:21.360
And he did that for all the kids too.
他对所有的孩子也都这样做。

872
00:44:21.480 --> 00:44:28.240
He made them feel special about whatever talent they had, whether it was art or music or anything.
他让他们觉得自己的天赋很特别，无论是艺术、音乐还是其他任何方面。

873
00:44:28.600 --> 00:44:35.820
He really embraced, you know, everyone's abilities, you know, as little as they were in that
他真的包容了每个人的能力，你知道，尽管在二年级的时候，

874
00:44:36.760 --> 00:44:38.360
moment as a second grader.
那些能力还很微小。

875
00:44:38.500 --> 00:44:39.460
But I'll never forget it.
但我永远不会忘记。

876
00:44:39.580 --> 00:44:41.400
Like being seen in that way.
那种被看见的感觉。

877
00:44:41.500 --> 00:44:41.980
Yeah, yeah, seen.
对，没错，被看见。

878
00:44:42.300 --> 00:44:42.580
Yeah.
嗯。

879
00:44:42.700 --> 00:44:44.520
And it was, it was, that was special.
那真的很特别。

880
00:44:44.640 --> 00:44:46.980
Something sort of clicked like, oh, that felt good.
某种东西好像突然通了，心想，哦，那种感觉真好。

881
00:44:47.660 --> 00:44:50.260
And so I guess I owe it all to him.
所以我猜我的一切都要归功于他。

882
00:44:50.840 --> 00:44:53.860
You know, Steve, I love that story because I have a similar story.
史蒂夫，我喜欢这个故事，因为我也有一个类似的故事。

883
00:44:54.040 --> 00:44:57.200
I took a summer school acting class and we had to do pantomime.
我上过一个暑期表演课，我们必须做哑剧。

884
00:44:57.600 --> 00:44:58.940
We had to mime things.
我们必须模拟各种事物。

885
00:44:58.940 --> 00:45:04.520
And my assignment was to go over to the refrigerator and get out some milk and pour it in a glass
我的作业是走到冰箱前，拿出一瓶牛奶，倒进杯子里，

886
00:45:04.520 --> 00:45:05.100
and drink it.
然后喝掉。

887
00:45:05.500 --> 00:45:06.720
And so I did it.
我就照做了。

888
00:45:06.860 --> 00:45:12.160
And afterwards, the teacher said, did everyone notice how Jenna shut the refrigerator with
事后，老师说，大家有没有注意到珍娜是用脚

889
00:45:12.160 --> 00:45:12.660
her foot?
关上冰箱门的？

890
00:45:12.920 --> 00:45:13.280
Oh!
噢！

891
00:45:13.520 --> 00:45:14.480
Yeah, it's exactly it.
对，就是那种感觉。

892
00:45:14.480 --> 00:45:18.460
And I was like, wait, I might be good at this because I didn't think.
我当时心想，等等，我可能擅长这个，因为我根本没多想。

893
00:45:18.920 --> 00:45:21.320
It just, I, maybe I have a little thing.
它就是……我，也许我在这方面有点天赋。

894
00:45:21.620 --> 00:45:22.200
Right, exactly.
没错，就是这样。

895
00:45:22.500 --> 00:45:26.200
You know, and then it just makes you feel like I might be good at this.
你知道，这会让你觉得我可能真的擅长这个。

896
00:45:26.200 --> 00:45:32.160
Or it might be something, you know, it might be something that I'm actually, yeah, capable
或者这可能是我，你知道，确实有能力做到的

897
00:45:32.160 --> 00:45:32.500
of.
事情。

898
00:45:32.660 --> 00:45:33.600
That's so fun.
那太有趣了。

899
00:45:33.800 --> 00:45:35.680
And it's not even like, look at me.
而且这甚至不是那种“快看我”的感觉。

900
00:45:35.880 --> 00:45:36.160
No.
没错。

901
00:45:36.920 --> 00:45:42.420
It's just a sense of, like one of the first senses of pride that you ever feel.
那是一种感觉，就像是你人生中第一次感受到的那种自豪感。

902
00:45:42.520 --> 00:45:42.760
Yes.
是的。

903
00:45:42.860 --> 00:45:47.460
That you accomplished something completely on your own, not your parents and nothing
你完全靠自己完成了一些事情，不是靠父母，也不是靠别人

904
00:45:47.460 --> 00:45:48.920
that you were instructed to do.
指导你去做的事。

905
00:45:49.040 --> 00:45:50.680
It's just something that you did naturally.
那纯粹是你自然而然做到的。

906
00:45:51.320 --> 00:45:55.300
And to have somebody notice it felt really good.
能有人注意到这一点，感觉真的很好。

907
00:45:55.300 --> 00:45:57.620
And that's a great teacher in my mind.
在我看来，那才是一位伟大的老师。

908
00:45:57.880 --> 00:45:59.660
Somebody who can pick up on those things.
能够敏锐察觉到这些细节的人。

909
00:46:00.100 --> 00:46:00.260
Yeah.
没错。

910
00:46:00.900 --> 00:46:01.300
Okay.
好的。

911
00:46:01.300 --> 00:46:02.500
I love this question.
我喜欢这个问题。

912
00:46:03.320 --> 00:46:04.060
Here it is.
就是这个。

913
00:46:04.180 --> 00:46:09.260
Are there any foods you don't eat anymore because of so many takes you had to do with
有没有什么食物是你因为拍摄时吃太多次，

914
00:46:09.260 --> 00:46:10.340
that certain food?
以至于现在再也不吃的？

915
00:46:10.340 --> 00:46:12.520
It can be on the office or any other job.
可以是《办公室》或者其他工作中的经历。

916
00:46:12.840 --> 00:46:13.940
I know mine right away.
我立刻就想到了我的那个。

917
00:46:14.200 --> 00:46:16.200
It's that cake that we had to eat.
就是我们必须吃的那种蛋糕。

918
00:46:16.320 --> 00:46:18.820
The ice cream cake for Meredith's birthday.
梅雷迪思生日时的冰淇淋蛋糕。

919
00:46:18.960 --> 00:46:20.000
Was it the Alliance?
是《联盟》那部戏吗？

920
00:46:20.000 --> 00:46:24.840
We started filming at 730 in the morning and we were given a slice of cake.
我们早上七点半开始拍摄，每人分到了一块蛋糕。

921
00:46:25.000 --> 00:46:27.660
And, you know, this is a rookie move on my part.
而且，你知道，那是我作为新手的失误。

922
00:46:27.800 --> 00:46:32.300
I took a plate with a big piece of cake and I took a huge bite in that first take.
我在第一个镜头里拿了一盘大块蛋糕，还咬了一大口。

923
00:46:32.580 --> 00:46:36.260
And by the end, my fork was shaking as it got close to my mouth because I was like,
到了最后，当叉子靠近嘴边时，我的手都在发抖，因为我在想，

924
00:46:36.800 --> 00:46:39.000
I can't, I can't eat it anymore.
我真的吃不下了，我再也吃不下去了。

925
00:46:39.580 --> 00:46:41.240
Anyway, I was asking you that.
总之，我刚才是在问你这个问题。

926
00:46:41.420 --> 00:46:45.780
But clearly I have a visceral, like, I can't eat ice cream cake anymore.
但显然我对此有种生理性的抗拒，就是我再也吃不了冰淇淋蛋糕了。

927
00:46:46.100 --> 00:46:46.800
That did it.
那次经历彻底毁了它。

928
00:46:46.800 --> 00:46:48.440
Yeah, it's so gross to me.
是啊，我觉得那太恶心了。

929
00:46:48.600 --> 00:46:50.940
I learned early on to nibble.
我早早就学会了要小口吃。

930
00:46:51.180 --> 00:46:51.560
Yeah.
没错。

931
00:46:52.080 --> 00:46:57.800
Not like if that first take, try not to take a big bite because then you have to match
如果是在第一个镜头，尽量别咬一大口，因为接下来你得在

932
00:46:57.800 --> 00:46:59.600
it for the rest of the day.
剩下的一整天里保持连贯性。

933
00:46:59.940 --> 00:47:00.120
Yeah.
是啊。

934
00:47:00.600 --> 00:47:03.220
Or I'm not hungry today.
或者说我今天不饿。

935
00:47:03.480 --> 00:47:05.480
You know, my character's not hungry.
你知道，我的角色不饿。

936
00:47:06.400 --> 00:47:09.180
But Michael would eat some pretty crazy stuff.
但迈克尔确实吃过一些相当疯狂的东西。

937
00:47:09.680 --> 00:47:15.060
Nothing that turned my stomach, nothing to the point where I can't eat it anymore.
没什么让我反胃到以后再也不敢吃的东西。

938
00:47:15.060 --> 00:47:21.440
I did a bit on The Daily Show where I was supposed to be eating Crisco.
我在《每日秀》里做过一个片段，当时我得吃起酥油。

939
00:47:21.780 --> 00:47:23.920
I was doing, it was some health food segment.
我当时在做一个健康食品的环节。

940
00:47:24.220 --> 00:47:26.660
And I said, well, this is all vegetable based.
我说，嗯，这全是植物基的。

941
00:47:26.920 --> 00:47:30.300
And I was like at the desk with John.
当时我和约翰坐在桌子后面。

942
00:47:30.900 --> 00:47:34.040
And so I take a big spoonful of Crisco.
然后我就舀了一大勺起酥油吃下去。

943
00:47:34.260 --> 00:47:35.200
For real Crisco?
真的起酥油？

944
00:47:35.420 --> 00:47:40.100
Well, they wanted to mock it up and just put like vanilla frosting in because no one would know.
嗯，他们本来想弄虚作假，放点香草糖霜进去，因为没人会发现。

945
00:47:40.100 --> 00:47:41.700
And I could play it like it's Crisco.
我也可以假装那是起酥油。

946
00:47:42.180 --> 00:47:47.920
But I said, no, leave Crisco in because that's going to be funnier for John to watch me eat Crisco.
但我说，不，就放真的起酥油，因为看我吃起酥油对约翰来说会更有趣。

947
00:47:48.140 --> 00:47:52.320
Because the expression on his face was pretty horrifying.
因为他当时脸上的表情简直惊恐万分。

948
00:47:52.760 --> 00:47:54.620
And I didn't practice it.
而且我之前没练习过。

949
00:47:54.720 --> 00:47:56.680
So I didn't know what I was getting myself into.
所以我根本不知道自己会遭什么罪。

950
00:47:57.020 --> 00:48:01.020
And so on camera, basically live, you know, that show is live to tape.
所以就在镜头前，基本上是现场直播的状态，你知道，那个节目是录播的。

951
00:48:01.020 --> 00:48:02.960
But it was really bad.
但那真的很糟糕。

952
00:48:03.240 --> 00:48:04.040
I did not.
我没有。

953
00:48:05.200 --> 00:48:05.840
I could.
我可以。

954
00:48:06.040 --> 00:48:10.440
I mean, the two of us had a hard time getting through the rest of that segment because he knew.
我是说，我们俩在那段节目的剩余部分里很难熬过去，因为他知道。

955
00:48:10.900 --> 00:48:12.680
He could see in your eye who were in pain.
他能从你的眼神里看出你在痛苦。

956
00:48:12.680 --> 00:48:12.820
He did.
他确实看出来了。

957
00:48:13.040 --> 00:48:14.260
He knew I was in distress.
他知道我很痛苦。

958
00:48:17.300 --> 00:48:20.260
So not that I would sit around eating Crisco.
所以并不是说我会坐在那儿吃起酥油。

959
00:48:20.540 --> 00:48:22.460
But that was a hard one.
但那次确实很难熬。

960
00:48:22.640 --> 00:48:22.800
Yeah.
是啊。

961
00:48:23.840 --> 00:48:25.000
Here's a good question.
这儿有个好问题。

962
00:48:25.240 --> 00:48:26.900
Do you have a Toby in your life?
你的生活中也有个“托比”吗？

963
00:48:27.360 --> 00:48:29.320
Someone who just enrages you.
那种让你火冒三丈的人。

964
00:48:29.580 --> 00:48:33.420
This was one of our favorite questions sent in.
这是我们收到的最喜欢的问题之一。

965
00:48:34.700 --> 00:48:37.500
Because we're like, we're sure Steve doesn't have a Toby.
因为我们当时想，我们肯定史蒂夫没有“托比”。

966
00:48:37.760 --> 00:48:38.860
But yeah.
但确实。

967
00:48:39.200 --> 00:48:41.500
Someone who just really for no reason.
那种真的毫无理由地。

968
00:48:41.640 --> 00:48:42.540
You just hate.
让你单纯讨厌的人。

969
00:48:43.720 --> 00:48:45.280
Just abject hatred.
纯粹的厌恶。

970
00:48:46.220 --> 00:48:47.560
No, I don't.
不，我没有。

971
00:48:47.560 --> 00:48:55.980
But that's one of my favorite character dynamics of the time that I spent on The Office.
但那是我在《办公室》期间最喜欢的角色互动之一。

972
00:48:56.420 --> 00:48:59.900
And it evolved from, I don't exactly.
它演变自……我也不太确定。

973
00:48:59.900 --> 00:49:05.480
I know it's been discussed as to, and I think the writers could sort of pinpoint clearly.
我知道有人讨论过这个问题，我想编剧们应该能很清楚地指出原因。

974
00:49:06.020 --> 00:49:16.480
But I think one day I just decided, I just got really mad at him and like disgusted by him for no reason.
但我记得有一天我突然决定，我就是莫名其妙地对他非常生气，甚至感到厌恶。

975
00:49:16.480 --> 00:49:19.360
And we just kind of stayed with it.
然后我们就一直保持这种设定了。

976
00:49:19.540 --> 00:49:20.860
People lost it.
大家都笑疯了。

977
00:49:21.120 --> 00:49:25.340
They would lose it at a table read when you would snap at Toby.
每次你在剧本围读会上对托比发火，他们都会笑得不行。

978
00:49:25.620 --> 00:49:28.720
We did an interview with Paul, and he said he remembers the moment.
我们采访过保罗，他说他记得那个瞬间。

979
00:49:28.900 --> 00:49:29.400
Oh, he does?
哦，他记得？

980
00:49:29.580 --> 00:49:34.780
It was during the alliance when he has to come in the office and sign Meredith's birthday card.
那是在“联盟”那一集，他得进办公室去签梅雷迪斯的生日贺卡。

981
00:49:35.200 --> 00:49:38.380
And apparently it just took him a long time.
显然他写得太慢了。

982
00:49:38.540 --> 00:49:39.060
Right.
没错。

983
00:49:39.060 --> 00:49:41.520
And you turned on him.
然后你就对他发难了。

984
00:49:42.220 --> 00:49:48.600
And you told him, you said, I don't know what it is, Paul, but I hate you for how long it's taken you to write this card.
你对他说，我不知道为什么，保罗，但我恨你写这张卡片写了这么久。

985
00:49:50.220 --> 00:49:54.580
And you guys laughed so hard about it.
你们当时笑得特别厉害。

986
00:49:55.000 --> 00:49:57.100
And you met like me as Michael.
你当时就像是迈克尔一样。

987
00:49:57.280 --> 00:50:00.520
Like Michael is so irritated with you right now.
就好像迈克尔现在对你非常恼火。

988
00:50:00.720 --> 00:50:01.780
And that was like.
当时就是那种感觉。

989
00:50:01.880 --> 00:50:03.680
Everyone else just signed their name.
其他人只是签了个名。

990
00:50:03.980 --> 00:50:05.600
But he started to write something.
但他开始写些什么。

991
00:50:05.600 --> 00:50:12.320
Yeah, I love the fact that it was never discussed, too, as to why and the specifics of it.
是啊，我也很喜欢这一点，就是从来没讨论过为什么，也没讨论过具体细节。

992
00:50:12.460 --> 00:50:12.640
Yeah.
是啊。

993
00:50:12.980 --> 00:50:14.760
And people have asked me that, too.
人们也问过我这个问题。

994
00:50:14.860 --> 00:50:16.940
Well, why does Michael hate Toby so much?
嗯，为什么迈克尔那么讨厌托比？

995
00:50:17.060 --> 00:50:22.060
And I think, well, I think it's like the end when our characters say goodbye to each other.
我想，嗯，我觉得就像最后我们的角色告别时那样。

996
00:50:23.520 --> 00:50:29.180
I'll never talk about exactly what was said, because that's like our thing.
我永远不会透露当时到底说了什么，因为那是我们之间的秘密。

997
00:50:29.300 --> 00:50:33.980
And it's more fun, too, to leave those things as sort of a mystery.
而且把这些事情留作某种谜团也更有趣。

998
00:50:34.160 --> 00:50:35.240
Like, well, why really?
比如，到底为什么呢？

999
00:50:35.640 --> 00:50:39.340
Because I built in Michael's head, like, all of the reasons.
因为我在迈克尔的脑海里构建了所有的理由。

1000
00:50:40.000 --> 00:50:41.920
But it's better that you don't know.
但你不知道反而更好。

1001
00:50:42.060 --> 00:50:42.320
Yeah.
是啊。

1002
00:50:42.400 --> 00:50:43.620
That it just kind of sits there.
它就那样静静地待在那里。

1003
00:50:44.020 --> 00:50:46.080
That's how I feel about the teapot letter.
我对那封茶壶信也是这种感觉。

1004
00:50:46.260 --> 00:50:46.440
Right.
没错。

1005
00:50:46.620 --> 00:50:48.720
Everyone wants to know what's in the teapot letter.
每个人都想知道茶壶信里写了什么。

1006
00:50:48.720 --> 00:50:54.340
And the thing is, is that what's in the teapot letter is what you need to be in the teapot letter.
关键在于，茶壶信里写的内容，必须是你需要它写的内容。

1007
00:50:54.680 --> 00:50:56.900
There's what I needed to be in the teapot letter.
那里面有我需要茶壶信里写的东西。

1008
00:50:57.040 --> 00:50:58.300
There's the thing that Pam needs.
那里面有帕姆需要的东西。

1009
00:50:59.480 --> 00:51:03.440
But, like, it has to be the most wonderful romantic thing.
但是，它必须是最美妙、最浪漫的东西。

1010
00:51:03.760 --> 00:51:05.160
And you can't write it.
而且你没法写出来。

1011
00:51:05.300 --> 00:51:07.320
Like, you can't tell people what was in it.
就像，你不能告诉别人里面写了什么。

1012
00:51:07.560 --> 00:51:08.880
It'll just be a letdown.
那只会让人失望。

1013
00:51:08.900 --> 00:51:09.480
It will.
确实会。

1014
00:51:09.680 --> 00:51:11.180
It can't live up to it, right?
它没法达到预期，对吧？

1015
00:51:11.340 --> 00:51:12.980
So it's like, I know what it said.
所以我知道它写了什么。

1016
00:51:13.240 --> 00:51:13.640
Completely.
完全知道。

1017
00:51:13.800 --> 00:51:17.060
But it's better if you write it yourself as an audience member.
但作为观众，你自己去解读会更好。

1018
00:51:17.060 --> 00:51:20.620
I think the writers in general felt that way about the show.
我觉得编剧们对这部剧整体也是这种感觉。

1019
00:51:20.720 --> 00:51:22.840
And I think Greg was very savvy about that.
而且我觉得格雷格对此非常有远见。

1020
00:51:23.000 --> 00:51:27.920
That you don't have to tell the whole story and just let people come to it.
你不需要讲完整个故事，只要让观众自己去领会就行。

1021
00:51:27.960 --> 00:51:31.640
And I think that's partly why people have liked the show over the years.
我觉得这也是为什么多年来人们一直喜欢这部剧的部分原因。

1022
00:51:31.740 --> 00:51:36.220
It doesn't spell everything out about these characters or about these relationships.
它没有把这些角色或关系的一切都说得明明白白。

1023
00:51:36.280 --> 00:51:39.960
You kind of acquire a knowledge over time.
你随着时间推移会慢慢了解。

1024
00:51:39.980 --> 00:51:41.860
And it becomes more ingrained that way.
这样反而更深入人心。

1025
00:51:41.860 --> 00:51:46.660
Anyway, this is sort of off the subject, but, you know, you talking about the teapot letter.
总之，这有点跑题了，但你提到了茶壶信。

1026
00:51:46.860 --> 00:51:51.600
And, you know, and I've thought about this many, many times since I left the show.
你知道，自从我离开剧组后，我思考过很多很多次。

1027
00:51:51.840 --> 00:51:58.540
That you and John, in my opinion, you and John were the rock of that show.
在我看来，你和约翰，你们俩是这部剧的基石。

1028
00:51:58.720 --> 00:52:03.360
Like, if not for your two characters, I feel like that show would have imploded.
如果不是你们两个角色，我觉得这部剧早就崩了。

1029
00:52:03.360 --> 00:52:09.040
Because there was so much craziness going on and so much wackiness.
因为当时剧里有太多疯狂和古怪的事情发生。

1030
00:52:09.460 --> 00:52:13.620
And you were both such grounded characters on that show.
而你们俩在剧中都是非常稳重的角色。

1031
00:52:13.820 --> 00:52:18.480
While simultaneously being incredibly funny and real.
同时又极其风趣且真实。

1032
00:52:18.480 --> 00:52:30.380
But the fact that everyone could bounce off of you and your very grounded, loving, and interesting relationship and how that grew.
大家都能在你们身上找到共鸣，你们那种稳重、充满爱意且有趣的关系，以及它是如何发展的，这些都很重要。

1033
00:52:30.820 --> 00:52:33.740
To me, that was the nucleus of the show.
对我来说，那是这部剧的核心。

1034
00:52:33.920 --> 00:52:35.260
I've always felt that way.
我一直都是这么认为的。

1035
00:52:35.500 --> 00:52:39.620
And the way that you guys, and you're both such good actors too.
而且你们俩，你们都是非常优秀的演员。

1036
00:52:39.620 --> 00:52:46.740
It's like, we're just so fortunate on so many fronts in terms of the casting of this show, in terms of the writing.
我们真的非常幸运，在选角和剧本方面都做得很好。

1037
00:52:47.260 --> 00:52:57.180
I attribute it to, well, to you guys, obviously, and to Greg for, like, putting this group of people together in just the right sort of alchemy.
我把这归功于你们，当然还有格雷格，他把这群人聚在一起，产生了奇妙的化学反应。

1038
00:52:57.380 --> 00:53:02.560
I don't know if I've ever specifically told you that, but I've always felt that way.
我不知道我是否专门跟你说过，但我一直都是这么想的。

1039
00:53:03.120 --> 00:53:04.660
Steve, thank you.
史蒂夫，谢谢你。

1040
00:53:05.460 --> 00:53:10.740
I remember when we got ready to shoot the pilot, Stephen and Ricky came to visit.
我记得我们准备拍试播集时，斯蒂芬和瑞奇来探班。

1041
00:53:11.060 --> 00:53:13.500
And we had, like, this one-day meeting with them.
我们和他们开了一天的会。

1042
00:53:13.760 --> 00:53:18.540
And Stephen told John and I, never forget you two are the heart of the show.
斯蒂芬告诉我和约翰，永远别忘了你们俩是这部剧的核心。

1043
00:53:19.080 --> 00:53:22.700
And I remember it was like in Threat Level Midnight.
我记得那就像在《午夜威胁》里一样。

1044
00:53:22.760 --> 00:53:23.600
I was like, gulp.
我当时想，咕咚。

1045
00:53:23.740 --> 00:53:24.100
Gulp.
咕咚。

1046
00:53:24.800 --> 00:53:27.880
I thought, I don't really know what that means.
我想，我不太明白那是什么意思。

1047
00:53:28.020 --> 00:53:29.380
That's sort of scary to me.
那对我来说有点可怕。

1048
